4
00:00:21,269 --> 00:00:23,670
Welcome to software engineering radio. I'm your host Pij Shamanut.

5
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:30,790
Today I will be discussing observability tool migration challenges with Yes Rabinovich also known as chess.

6
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:37,190
Chess is the CTO and co-founder at ground cover which provides full stack observability for Kubernetes.

7
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:41,990
Chess was previously the chief architect at the healthcare security company Cyber MDX.

8
00:00:41,990 --> 00:00:46,310
and spent 8 years in the cyber security division of the Israeli prime minister's office.

9
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:54,630
Chess is a repeat guest at software engineering radio having previously spoken about Kubernetes observability in episode 591.

10
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:56,310
Chess, welcome to the show.

11
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,070
Thank you. Thank you for having me.

12
00:00:58,079 --> 00:01:01,910
Today we'll be talking about what are the challenges.

13
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:06,550
and what are the techniques one uses to migrate observability tools.

14
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:09,910
Before we jump into the topics, I just wanted to touch on a few episodes.

15
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:14,710
where we have covered observability previously in software engineering radio.

16
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:17,830
So episode 556 where Alex Bolton spoke on open telemetry.

17
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:24,390
episode 57 where Kevin who spoke about data observability.

18
00:01:24,390 --> 00:01:29,670
and episode 675 where Brian Demers spoke on observability on the tool chain.

19
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:34,310
Quite good episodes to refer back to understand more details about observability.

20
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:41,109
Let's start with what drives the need for firms to migrate from the existing observability tool set.

21
00:01:41,109 --> 00:01:43,590
Do you have a story or an example.

22
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:47,670
where a firm needed to migrate from their existing observability tool set?

23
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,670
Yeah, actually a lot.

24
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:53,749
Basically every customer of ground cover not all of them.

25
00:01:53,759 --> 00:01:58,709
but most of them are coming from some legacy vendors.

26
00:01:58,709 --> 00:02:01,990
they most R&amp;Ds already use observability platform.

27
00:02:01,990 --> 00:02:07,830
So when they want to move to the new observability platform more modern.

28
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:10,229
that rely on bring your own cloud and EVPF.

29
00:02:10,229 --> 00:02:18,150
the main challenge is how do we migrate all the hard work we did as an R&amp;D organization.

30
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:20,390
right.

31
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:24,550
you can think about it as dashboards right.

32
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:31,030
most of R&amp;D has some kind of top 10 top 20 we often seen hundreds of dashboards across.

33
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:34,391
the entire R&amp;D. It could be monitors.

34
00:02:34,391 --> 00:02:39,830
So imagine that you rely on specific alerts to wake you up at night to make.

35
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:46,229
sure your software is actually behaving as as you expect it to be.

36
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:48,949
and that your customers get their SLAs's.

37
00:02:48,949 --> 00:02:53,830
And it also could be integrations that you did or even observability pipeline configurations.

38
00:02:53,840 --> 00:03:02,390
All those configurations and integrations have been in the work for maybe 5 10 years from different people.

39
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,910
in the organization.

40
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:07,589
Some of them already left.

41
00:03:07,589 --> 00:03:11,110
So even the knowledge of what we have is sometimes missing.

42
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:17,670
So all that makes the decision to move forward to new observability platform a lot more challenging.

43
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:20,950
Right? So it is a challenging problem.

44
00:03:20,959 --> 00:03:23,509
But what is the primary driver?

45
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:28,710
Do you have an example where a customer actually came to you to ground cover and said.

46
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:32,630
because of these reasons we wanted to move from our legacy observability tool set?

47
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:43,750
Yeah, we recently had a customer with a thousand monitors which they didn't know how they built it.

48
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:48,229
So the people that actually created those monitors are no longer in the organization.

49
00:03:48,229 --> 00:03:53,990
So imagine how frightening it is to now trying to migrate those monitors without knowing.

50
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,631
if what you're doing is actually the right thing. There is no way.

51
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:07,110
So let's take a simple example of think about monitors that alert when specific log happens.

52
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:09,750
It could be like a free text search.

53
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:12,309
It could be specific attribute with specific value.

54
00:04:12,309 --> 00:04:15,990
Let's say status error.

55
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:18,629
So now when you migrate those monitors,

56
00:04:18,629 --> 00:04:21,110
imagine that you have a mistake and basically you don't know.

57
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:26,151
that you are not covered by your monitors. So that's pretty scary.

58
00:04:26,151 --> 00:04:32,310
This specific customer, their requirement was to have a fully automated process.

59
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:37,110
that will make sure their monitors are being migrated successfully.

60
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:42,230
And in order to do that, it actually it goes really deep.

61
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:47,670
You need to understand the log structure and the transformation that each log is going through.

62
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:53,430
in order to ensure that those monitors are still covering you.

63
00:04:53,430 --> 00:04:54,950
and basically that you can sleep good at night.

64
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,030
Thousand monitors, that's quite a lot.

65
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,950
Is that the norm?

66
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:04,070
Do you have quite a few customers with that many dashboards and monitors?

67
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:10,629
I think the average customer has few hundreds of monitors and at least dozens of dashboards.

68
00:05:10,629 --> 00:05:16,950
I think it really depends if the customers are using infrastructure as code to generate those monitors.

69
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:20,230
because it's usually very hard to create it manually.

70
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:24,070
But yeah, we're seeing numbers of hundreds of monitors.

71
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:26,870
We have customer with 5,000 monitors.

72
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:29,830
That's the high bar. Like I think the biggest we have is 10,000 monitors.

73
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,990
Wow.

74
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:34,629
If you have taken all that effort to build thousand monitors.

75
00:05:34,629 --> 00:05:39,270
and you know that migrating is going to be quite challenging, why go into that effort?

76
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:44,230
What was the reason of saying that this does no longer works for me?

77
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:50,469
Yeah, that's a good question and it comes back to the reason why we started ground cover.

78
00:05:50,469 --> 00:05:56,710
We were on the other side using some legacy vendor and basically you have two main challenges.

79
00:05:56,710 --> 00:06:00,870
One is that you need to generate the data in order to have all the data.

80
00:06:00,870 --> 00:06:03,189
and you need to instrument your application.

81
00:06:03,189 --> 00:06:08,550
I'm talking about APM, open telemetry or whatever SDK you're using.

82
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:12,950
That's really cool, but you don't know what you don't know.

83
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:20,230
So if you didn't instrument your application, you basically don't have the information that actually what's going on.

84
00:06:20,230 --> 00:06:22,950
That's one reason you want more data.

85
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:29,430
And the other reason that it's very very expensive old way of doing observability is.

86
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:33,270
basically send all the data to some SAS provider.

87
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:38,390
But the reality is that it's very expensive not just even from licensing fee.

88
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:43,990
Think about the egress fee that you need to pay in order to send all.

89
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:48,390
that data and that lead to some kind of a vicious cycle.

90
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:50,550
So before that we said we want more data right.

91
00:06:50,550 --> 00:06:52,870
and then we instrument our application we get more data.

92
00:06:52,870 --> 00:06:57,670
and then the bill come and it's very very expensive.

93
00:06:57,670 --> 00:07:02,309
So now we are reducing the data that we just instrumented and that caused frustration.

94
00:07:02,309 --> 00:07:05,589
and most organization have roles in the R&amp;D.

95
00:07:05,589 --> 00:07:10,150
or devops that main concern is to make sure observability cost is under control.

96
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:16,950
What we built in ground cover is the first bring your own cloud platform observability platform.

97
00:07:16,950 --> 00:07:23,430
which means that the data plane is inside the customer account.

98
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:29,029
So imagine that we provision a new AWS account or GCP or Azure whatever hyperscaler you're using.

99
00:07:29,029 --> 00:07:31,350
We deploy the back end.

100
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:36,309
We have a control plane that manage it but all the data plane is inside the customer environment.

101
00:07:36,309 --> 00:07:40,469
which means it's a lot more efficient.

102
00:07:40,469 --> 00:07:45,830
and it allow us basically not to charge our customers by volume of data.

103
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:53,830
So that also help in terms of the anxiety that most customer have around pricing. So that's one thing.

104
00:07:53,830 --> 00:08:00,310
and the other thing that we did is we have our own ebpf sensor. which generates a lot more data.

105
00:08:00,319 --> 00:08:05,350
and because the customer doesn't pay by volume those two new techniques are.

106
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:10,150
actually amplifying each other in a very nice way right.

107
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:16,070
so if I understood it correctly the primary reason for firms aspiring to migrate from the existing observability.

108
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,309
tool set is that.

109
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:21,909
most of the legacy observability tool set relied on a lot of data.

110
00:08:21,919 --> 00:08:28,070
and having that much data made it very expensive.

111
00:08:28,070 --> 00:08:34,070
Whereas new tool sets like ground cover are able to not send the data to your particular cloud.

112
00:08:34,070 --> 00:08:40,469
Whereas you can use your the firm's own cloud or use it on prem which reduces the cost.

113
00:08:40,479 --> 00:08:46,230
and that's one of the primary drivers for firms aspiring to migrate out. Is that right?

114
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:48,070
That's correct.

115
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:54,230
And even more than that, so because we're not making money out of volume,

116
00:08:54,230 --> 00:08:58,949
that liberate the product team to basically be on the customer side, right?

117
00:08:58,949 --> 00:09:03,430
So imagine that you're trying to sell an observability platform and you make money out of data.

118
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:05,750
and you see the customer sending garbage data, right?

119
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:10,230
We all know the phrase like garbage data in, garbage out.

120
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:17,350
But from a seller perspective, if you're making money from that garbage data, you're in a conflict, right?

121
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:21,829
Like we all want to believe that the vendor will encourage the customer to stop sending it.

122
00:09:21,829 --> 00:09:27,190
but the reality is that they have zero incentive and actually hurt their business.

123
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:29,670
So when we started ground cover we decided.

124
00:09:29,670 --> 00:09:35,350
that we will always be on the customer side in terms of value.

125
00:09:35,350 --> 00:09:41,190
So our product team is constantly trying to reduce the infrastructure cost.

126
00:09:41,190 --> 00:09:47,430
and making sure the customer have the right data that he needs.

127
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:49,509
So think about like a simple example.

128
00:09:49,509 --> 00:09:54,870
You have an observability pipeline that drop logs by pattern by specific pattern that it's spammy.

129
00:09:54,870 --> 00:09:56,870
You have two options to do that, right?

130
00:09:56,870 --> 00:10:03,829
The old way, the legacy way is to make the customer send the data to the SAS platform.

131
00:10:03,829 --> 00:10:06,070
and charge by ingest which could be very very expensive.

132
00:10:06,070 --> 00:10:12,150
In ground cover, we push down the filters to the sensor.

133
00:10:12,150 --> 00:10:14,710
So basically the sensor at the node level will not send those logs.

134
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:20,630
because we don't make money of it anyway.

135
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:25,910
So why would we waste time and energy and money by serializing those logs,

136
00:10:25,910 --> 00:10:27,910
shipping it over network and then dropping it at the other edge?

137
00:10:27,910 --> 00:10:31,350
Because we're doing the same thing from both sides.

138
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:37,829
We can just drop it at the node level and reduce a lot of cost and energy.

139
00:10:37,829 --> 00:10:40,310
So that's just one example of why this alignment matters,

140
00:10:40,310 --> 00:10:46,389
why the new model actually matters to all our customers.

141
00:10:46,389 --> 00:10:51,350
And what are some of the typical pain points you have seen during the migration process?

142
00:10:51,350 --> 00:10:55,269
and if you can maybe bring it to life with some any examples that might come to mind?

143
00:10:55,279 --> 00:10:57,430
Yeah, sure.

144
00:10:57,430 --> 00:11:02,949
There's something that we called in the company observability for observability.

145
00:11:02,949 --> 00:11:05,829
So what it means is that the customer want to migrate, right?

146
00:11:05,829 --> 00:11:15,430
But they have 50 60 integrations active and they don't even know what integrations they have, right?

147
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:22,310
because most of the integrations are configured somewhere else and they just send data.

148
00:11:22,310 --> 00:11:28,230
and it's been done by dozens of people across the organization.

149
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:30,550
So we had customer with I think it was 40.

150
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:35,430
or 50 integrations with different cloud providers and different configurations.

151
00:11:35,430 --> 00:11:41,430
and basically what we did in the migration process is we first start with discovery of what data.

152
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:43,750
you have.

153
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:48,949
So it's looking at observability data from an observability perspective in a way.

154
00:11:48,949 --> 00:11:52,069
So we inspect the data, analyze it.

155
00:11:52,069 --> 00:11:57,110
and say okay we found 50 active integrations according to the data that we're seeing.

156
00:11:57,110 --> 00:12:02,550
and now we automatically translate each and every integration from each.

157
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:08,150
and every vendor to basically adapt to how it looks at ground cover integration.

158
00:12:08,150 --> 00:12:15,030
So the customer get like a report displaying those are the integrations that we found.

159
00:12:15,030 --> 00:12:20,710
those were the integrations that will be added and just click next and that will be done.

160
00:12:20,710 --> 00:12:26,150
So think about how much hard work you have to do just to get all those configurations from.

161
00:12:26,150 --> 00:12:28,949
an existing integrations you have.

162
00:12:28,949 --> 00:12:34,230
This is almost impossible to do manually without mistakes.

163
00:12:34,230 --> 00:12:41,030
So that's like one simple aspect that actually starts the process of migration from most of our customers.

164
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:43,110
Yep.

165
00:12:43,110 --> 00:12:45,110
Sounds very complicated and difficult task.

166
00:12:45,110 --> 00:12:49,509
Let's move to the next section where we discuss on approaches of migration.

167
00:12:49,509 --> 00:12:52,310
So what approaches have you seen firms take for migration?

168
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:57,509
Is it you know phased approach or is it a big bang approach.

169
00:12:57,509 --> 00:12:59,670
and are there other approaches that you have seen as well?

170
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:06,550
That's a really good question and we. actually we have our own opinion about what you should do first and how.

171
00:13:06,550 --> 00:13:11,350
but we also very adaptive to what the customers want.

172
00:13:11,350 --> 00:13:18,310
because sometimes the organizational needs are much more important than what we think are the right way.

173
00:13:18,310 --> 00:13:20,870
but usually what happened is we first start with discovery.

174
00:13:20,870 --> 00:13:23,110
So we will fetch all your assets,

175
00:13:23,110 --> 00:13:29,509
dashboards, white, log pipelines, even policies in terms of user management.

176
00:13:29,509 --> 00:13:32,310
and permissions and we first just visualize it.

177
00:13:32,310 --> 00:13:36,550
It's like getting you on boarded on what are we going to do here?

178
00:13:36,550 --> 00:13:40,069
Those are our tasks.

179
00:13:40,069 --> 00:13:44,710
From this point, you have a few options.

180
00:13:44,710 --> 00:13:47,829
We usually encourage to start by the most important assets.

181
00:13:47,829 --> 00:13:50,470
So how do we know what's important?

182
00:13:50,470 --> 00:13:53,670
we can get some metadata.

183
00:13:53,670 --> 00:14:00,389
So you can imagine that dashboards that are being edited in the recent week.

184
00:14:00,389 --> 00:14:02,550
or month are probably more important.

185
00:14:02,550 --> 00:14:05,430
than dashboard that haven't been touched for more than a year.

186
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:13,431
So we get that information and also we can get information like how many views do you have. for that dashboards.

187
00:14:13,431 --> 00:14:19,030
and basically create some kind of a prioritization of the assets.

188
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:22,069
So monitors that are firing right now.

189
00:14:22,069 --> 00:14:27,509
or fired in the last week are probably critical for organization because they're actually happening, right?

190
00:14:27,509 --> 00:14:32,230
If there is a monitor that never been fired in the last year, it's probably less important.

191
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:37,750
So that's one view that most of our customers find really really effective.

192
00:14:37,750 --> 00:14:44,470
But we have another view which is focus around what's missing.

193
00:14:44,470 --> 00:14:47,671
So let's say we miss some metrics. We got like 5,000 metrics.

194
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:54,390
We mapped them and we fetched 4,900, right? So, we're missing 100 metrics.

195
00:14:54,399 --> 00:15:01,509
Now, how do you know which one is important?

196
00:15:01,509 --> 00:15:04,470
So, basically what we do is we fetch again all the assets.

197
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:07,430
that use those metrics or those signals.

198
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:14,550
and then we rank them by how popular they are, by the usage.

199
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:19,911
So if there is one metric that is missing to accomplish 80% of the dashboard, let's focus on that.

200
00:15:19,911 --> 00:15:27,670
So basically those are the two angles that we're trying to attack that this hard process.

201
00:15:27,670 --> 00:15:30,390
And the main thing is to build trust, right? We we start this process.

202
00:15:30,399 --> 00:15:33,030
Some say it's like moving a bank.

203
00:15:33,030 --> 00:15:34,710
Some say it's like moving an apartment.

204
00:15:34,710 --> 00:15:36,629
I don't know which size I am.

205
00:15:36,629 --> 00:15:41,030
But the common thing is it's very stressful and there is a lot of fear around it.

206
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:47,430
And our job is to give you the confidence we can do it.

207
00:15:47,430 --> 00:15:50,949
So we bring the tools to help you analyze what is going on,

208
00:15:50,949 --> 00:15:57,350
what is the progress, what's needed to achieve more progress.

209
00:15:57,350 --> 00:16:01,990
And the last part is the feedback loop that is very very important.

210
00:16:01,990 --> 00:16:07,829
So if you added that missing integration, you automatically visualize the data.

211
00:16:07,829 --> 00:16:10,629
you automatically see that those box checked.

212
00:16:10,629 --> 00:16:13,030
and that's those kind of little games.

213
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:16,949
that help you make progress in this quest build the confidence that we can do it together.

214
00:16:16,959 --> 00:16:22,310
and the last piece is bring all the all our R&amp;D like all the stakeholders.

215
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:26,310
and present them the process the results of what we did.

216
00:16:26,310 --> 00:16:27,910
and let them play with it and give the feedback.

217
00:16:27,910 --> 00:16:31,829
that's how we usually close a P or on boarding of a new customer.

218
00:16:31,839 --> 00:16:38,630
and do you have any story or example where a particular migration did not go as per plan. What were the challenges?

219
00:16:38,639 --> 00:16:42,390
What went wrong? And how did you fix it?

220
00:16:42,399 --> 00:16:52,710
I think the most challenging part is getting all the observability pipelines right.

221
00:16:52,710 --> 00:16:54,870
because usually when you work with customers,

222
00:16:54,870 --> 00:16:59,509
you see they have some kind of layers of software in their organization, right?

223
00:16:59,509 --> 00:17:02,790
We all have this service that we know we should fix.

224
00:17:02,790 --> 00:17:06,949
but or we should change those log lines but no one is getting to it.

225
00:17:06,949 --> 00:17:08,949
and it's pretty scary because it's an old monolith.

226
00:17:08,949 --> 00:17:15,270
that 80% of the company rely on and nobody want to touch it.

